WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.910
With one of the biggest reality shows on television,
the Pawn Stars crew has struck pawn shop paydirt

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.960
more times than even they can count.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.820
But not every deal they make on the show is
a perfect play, and when they get ripped off,

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.440
they get ripped off big time.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.420
Here are the worst moments when the Pawn Stars
got seriously cheated.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:20.540
Shoeless Joe Jackson fake

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.760
In the season six episode "Say It Ain't So,"
Rick shelled out $13,000 on a book he believed

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.700
might contain the authentic signature of baseball
great "Shoeless" Joe Jackson.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.580
Rick couldn't have been more excited during
the appraisal:

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.000
"Do you know how rare it is to find his signature?"

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.120
"No, not at all."

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.800
"It's the rarest sports signature, period."

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.860
But despite the seller's questionable certificate
of authenticity, and Rick's own reservations…

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.020
"Of all the signatures in the world this is
the most faked."

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.270
...he took a gamble and bought it anyway.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:56.670
After shelling out the cash, Rebecca, his
book expert, told him that the signature was

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:57.520
likely a fake.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:03.460
So Rick then sent it out to another authenticator
who reiterated the bad news: not only was

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.940
it a forgery, it was a laughably bad one.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.060
So much for that home run, Rick.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.060
Willie Mays strike-out

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:16.740
In a 2012 episode, Corey made the blunder
of forking over $31,000 for what he believed

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.740
was a game-worn Willie Mays San Francisco
Giants uniform from 1961.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.300
But the red flags with this one were apparent
from the jump.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.020
For starters, the uniform was pristine—which
Chumlee noticed right away:

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.740
"This doesn't look game-worn.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.110
Willie Mays was a badass, he was sliding around
on the dirt and the grass.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.540
I'd imagine there'd be a bunch of stains on
it."

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:37.960
Good observation, Chum.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:43.580
Also, the seller had no authentication paperwork
whatsoever, which is never a good sign.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.420
But Big Hoss took a gamble and boy, was he
all kinds of wrong.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:53.680
Not only did Gold &amp; Silver Pawn fail to retail
it at a ridiculous asking price of $80,000,

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:59.820
but they only ended up getting only $19,200
for it at auction around two years later,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.680
a nearly $12,000 loss.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.360
And it gets worse.

00:02:03.369 --> 00:02:07.810
Hauls of Shame reported that the uniform was
never worn in a game by Mays, because it never

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.090
even belonged to Mays.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.310
That technically means Corey didn't get as
ripped off as he could have, but we seriously

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.440
doubt he's happy with how that deal went down.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.640
"School's in session.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:17.940
You just got schooled."

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.000
Wells Fargo heist

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.110
In a fifth season episode called "Corey's
Big Play," Rick made the unusual mistake of

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:30.540
buying something he was unsure of: a 19th-century
Wells Fargo strongbox containing an old prison

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:31.380
ball and chain.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.700
And "unsure" is putting it lightly.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.820
"Ok, What are you trying to say?"

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:37.400
"It's fake."

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:43.840
He still dropped $450 on the box, only to
have his hopes of a profit shot down by expert

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.840
and show regular Mark "The Beard of Knowledge"
Hall-Patton,

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.080
"This is a complete fantasy piece.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:51.920
It's a complete fake."

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:53.760
"G------- Rick"

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.520
Leave it up to the Old Man to rub it in.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:58.080
Auction losses

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:03.069
If the World Famous Gold and Silver Pawn Shop
can't retail an item, they always have the

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:04.849
option of sending it to auction.

00:03:04.849 --> 00:03:09.750
That's what Rick tried to do with several
big ticket items including a 1940s Indian

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:14.801
Motorcycle with a sidecar and a Vic Flick
owned and played Fender Stratocaster, which

00:03:14.801 --> 00:03:17.800
he put on the block at Julien's Auctions in
Los Angeles.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:24.640
Unfortunately, he took a hit for "$29,000
on the motorcycle, and $35,000 on the guitar."

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:25.640
Ouch.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.159
He must not have done well with his other
items either, because he reported his total

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.320
loss at around $100,000.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.700
"Sold most of my items, I'm getting my ass
handed to me.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.740
Ahh, it doesn't feel good."

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.320
Blindsided by the bayou

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.200
When the History Channel launched Cajun Pawn
Stars in 2012, the boys from Las Vegas apparently

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.000
felt blindsided by the move.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:54.160
According to TMZ, the network had promised
no spin-offs would be produced.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.060
The History Channel called the new addition
a "southern spin" on the original, and they

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.120
weren't kidding.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.300
"I got some gator, I got some raccoon.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:02.460
Which one we gonna cook?"

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.700
Neither the network nor the Harrisons commented
publicly on the tension, but former Pawn Stars

00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:12.650
manager Wayne Jefferies said he was the mole
that talked to TMZ, and he said he got fired

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:13.150
for it.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:17.400
Looks like in this case, Pawn Stars didn't
get ripped off by a customer — they got

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.980
ripped off by their own network.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:20.140
"See ya later, dork."

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:21.589
Thanks for watching!

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:24.349
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:24.349 --> 00:04:26.800
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

